Novák helped Trabzonspor to second place, Škoda also scored
Defender Filip Novák helped with a goal in the final round of Turkey's Trabzonspor football league to the title of vice-champion.
His team won 2:1 on Kayserispor's course and kept third Besiktas behind.
The striker Milan Škoda scored his 10th goal for Rizespor after the January move from Slavia, which he saved, although he succumbed 1:3 on the Fenerbahce course.
Novák confirmed his excellent productivity at the defender with his seventh goal in this league year.
In the 63rd minute, goalkeeper Kayserispore only fired a sharp shot and the Czech footballer from an angle hit the ball into the net.
The homers managed to equalize five minutes before the end, but Trabzonspor pulled the victory to his side in the sixth minute of the set-up when he successfully finished a four-on-on-one break.
In the beginning of the second half of the match with Fenerbahce, he equalized after a 1:1 solo.
Rizespor did not maintain the indecisive state for a long time, but he did not have to regret it, because thanks to the results of his opponents in a row he remained three points above the descending zone.
The competition will end with Sunday's duel of the champion Basaksehir on Kasimpasa's course.
The well-hidden Cave by the hermit in the woods near Nový Bor was actually not habitable
In the north of Bohemia in the foothills of the Lusatian Mountains, near Nové Bor in the Českolipsko region, in the forests through which Hraběcí cesta led, there are several sandstone rock formations.
Natural and artificially created.
And we'll go after that human hand created.
The place is called the Cave by the hermit.
In reality, however, there has never been a hermit, and the cave served as a shelter, more precisely a hideout chamber apparently for grain.
The cave is carved from the sidege to the rocket and accessible by stairs from above.
However, it is practically not visible until the last moment.
It will be revealed only by narrow stairs leading to a rock gallery, from where it is just entering the rock.
Although the chamber appears to be quite far from human dwellings, at the time of its inception there may have been fields on the plateau, where there is a forest.
"The fact that the object does not have a window, and even in a traditional place located a fireplace, unequivocally proves that no hermit here could ever survive the winter," explains historian Kamil Podroužek from the Faculty of Arts of Jan Evangelista Purkyně in Ústí nad Labem.
The single-space building has only a stone bench at the back, apparently used for storing containers.
"I see it as a chamber for storing some food or products from the field," adds Kamil Podroužek.
There are signs of tools on the walls of the cave.
Breaking off the sanstock with a canine was quite simple and quick for the insiders.
It was enough to use joints appropriately and gradually debunk the sandstone.
Depending on the length of the canine trace and the method of chopping in general, it is possible to determine the period of formation of the cave.
According to the historian, it is probably the end of the 18th century.
The cave is freely accessible, and according to the mess that appears in it, someone sometimes sleeps there.
If you approach the cave from above from one of the forest paths and you will be impressed under the cave by a flowing stream and a nearby county path, you will definitely find a trail down.
And on the way out of the forest, you may still be captivated by the nearby Raven Rocks.
Jockey Minarik is in an artificial sleep after a heavy fall in Germany
"Filip's in the best hands right now.
The institute is equipped for similar cases, and fortunately we have part of the family in Hanover.
This greatly eases the situation and also the care of my daughter Finja," news server Jezdci.cz was quoted as saying by Minařík's wife Katji.
"Everyone's doing their best, Filip needs time for convalescence right now.
There's no point in giving any prognosis.
Anyone who has ever encountered the issue of head traumas and patients in artificial sleep knows that the situation can change.
Philip's a great fighter.
We all have to be patient now and give him time," the wife of the forty-five-year-old jockey said.
Frankie Dettori, who won his seventh King George VI and Queen Elizabeth Stakes race on Saturday, also called for financial support for the injured Minarik on Twitter, equalling Lester Piggott's legend.
Nearly 70 thousand euros have been collected on the Gofundme server today.
The target is an amount of 100 thousand euros (2.6 million crowns).
Video referee took Sassuola's footballers' four goals, losing 0:2
Sassuola's footballers got the ball into their opponent's goal four times in Saturday's Napoli game, but in the end they had to reconcile with a 0:2 defeat.
None of their goals were acknowledged by the referees after consulting the video because of offside.
According to Italian media, this is a record adversity of technology, so many interventions have never been thwarted by VAR by one team.
"VAR was stricter on Sassuolo than the road radar and punished him like no team yet.
The new technology breaks records," Gazzetta dello Sport wrote in a commentary.
"Festival of unrecognized Sassuola goals.
VAR has never been such strict for one team," added Corriere dello Sport.
Napoli took the lead as early as the 8-minute on Saturday, but after half an hour of play, Sassuolo appeared to have equalized.
However, Filip Djuričiĸ’s goal was not acknowledged after the video referee’s intervention because of the offside.
For the same reason, Djuričić ́s other interventions did not pay, and Francesco Caputa and Domenico Berardi.
On the contrary, the home side added one more goal.
Electric cars are reporting a new record.
In the Czech Republic, they recharged the most in history
"The growth was driven by a 30 percent increase in the number of stations in our network and the trend of more frequent charging at public stations, as well as the accelerated development of registrations of new e-cars," said Tomáš Chmelík, head of ČEZ's clean technology department.
Other big players, such as PRE and E.ON, electric car maker Tesla, as well as Billa and Lidl supermarkets, also subscribe to the market.
The operators of classic petrol stations are represented by MOL.
She doesn’t have numbers yet.
"We only started building our network for electromobility last year," company spokeswoman Jana Jusková explained.
However, the editors of the Journal exclusively obtained data from all 216 electrical stands operated by CEZ.
It shows that drivers removed an average of 13.5 kWh from the stations.
This corresponds to the range of about a hundred kilometers of a smaller electric car.
Apart from small pumps up to 1 kWh, the average charging takes two and a half hours.
The vast majority of refueling took place in a free tariff.
If motorists were already paying for the subscription, their typical spending was around 330 crowns.
Electromobility pulls mainly in the capital.
There are all five busiest chargers of the ČEZ company on the territory of Prague, which the entire regional towns.
Olomouc leads among the regions.
On the contrary, the slightest interest is in remote stations in the border area, for example in Bohumín or Prachatice, which often remains unused throughout the day.
More than four thousand electric cars are currently registered in the Czech Republic.
In the first half of this year alone, 1274 new e-cars were added.
In a wide-ranging poll of "The Czechs on the Movement", eight percent of readers said they were considering buying an electric car into the family.
However, only one-third of respondents know where to look for the nearest charging station in their area.
Not just for better water retention.
Environmental activists protested in Prague
According to the organizers, the government’s actions so far on the protection and recovery of the Czech landscape and the resolution of the climatic crisis are insufficient, the speakers have called on the state to take a stronger approach.
Representatives of major organizations such as the Rainbow Movement, Greenpeace and the Green Party, as well as smaller local associations, took part in the event titled The Last Drop.
"For example, we have people who have been speaking out for a long time against the expansion on the Turów mine, there are people who speak out against gravelift mining near Uherský Ostroh, there are people who oppose the construction of nonsensical dams," Marika Volfová said on behalf of the organizers.
It stated that the main demands of the protesters are the creation of a plan for water retention in the landscape, a sharp change in agricultural policy and a system of subsidies towards promoting sustainable agriculture and, as quickly as possible, the transition from fossil fuels to renewable energy sources.
"The government is supposed to defend people's interests, not a ruthless and devastating business," she said.
There was a stall on Malostranský Square with a petition calling on representatives of the Czech state to contribute to the reduction of greenhouse gas emissions and the restoration of Czech soil and landscape.
The people present were equipped with banners with slogans such as "The end of rapeseed depression" or "Less coal, more newts".
From South Moravia, Jan Škoda came to Prague from the association Behind the Water for People, which protests against the plan of gravel mining in the Hodonín region.
"It endangers the source of drinking water for 140 000 people," he said.
He added that wells represent the fifth most abundant source of drinking water in the Czech Republic.
According to him, the planned extraction of gravel is located only about 500 meters from the wells, and according to estimates, it is certain that the quality of the water will be compromised.
Vojtěch Veselý came for the so-called agri-ecological farmers from the village of Valeč in the Karlovy Vary region.
He drew attention to the positives of small farms that don't marry for yields and profits and supply residents right there.
"Small agri-ecological farms are able to feed the entire population quite normally, in doing so, they do not do such destruction on the form of the countryside and the environment," he said.
Three people were killed in a plane crash on a house in Germany
Three people died in the crash of an ultra-light plane into an apartment building in West Germany today and two others were injured.
This was reported by local emergency services, according to which there are probably two crew members and one occupant of the house among the dead.
The plane crashed onto the roof of a building in Wesel in North Rhine-Westphalia.
The attic apartment, which was one of the five housing units in the house, was subsequently engulfed by the fire.
Firefighters have already managed to bring the flames under control.
Robert Meyboom, the deputy commander of the local fire department, said both members of the plane's crew were believed to have died.
The third victim, according to police, is a woman who lived in the house.
Westdeutsche Allgemeine Zeitung (WAZ), citing the mayor of Wesela, wrote that it was a single mother whose two-year-old daughter was rescued from the apartment with slight injuries.
In the vicinity of the accident, an emergency parachute was found, which apparently served as a brake when landing.
Police say it came from a crashed aircraft.
There was room for two people in the aircraft.
The machine took off from an airport in the nearby town of Marl.
The Rheinische Post, citing eyewitnesses, said the plane collided with a hot-air balloon before the crash.
However, this information has not yet been confirmed.
WAZ, citing other witnesses, wrote that the aircraft had disintegrated in the air before hitting the ground.
On Saturday, Česká spořitelna erroneously charged some card payments twice.
She's already eliminated the error
Česká spořitelna erronely charged its clients some card payments twice on Saturday afternoon.
According to bank spokesperson Lukáš Kropík, the problem may have involved thousands of transactions.
She had already eliminated the mistake and returned the money to her clients.
Problems with the double billing of card payments were experienced by the savings bank on Saturday afternoon.
"Just yesterday (Saturday) last night, we made an update to the system and immediately returned all of these double payments to our clients.
At the moment, everything works without any problems," Kropík said on Sunday afternoon.
The exact number of clients to whom the bank deducted money from card payments twice does not know, according to its estimate was lower thousands of transactions.
The causes of the savings bank's problems are being determined.
"It could have been a combination of multiple factors," Kropík said.
Česká spořitelna is the largest bank in the Czech Republic according to the number of clients.
At the end of March, it had 4.6 million.
The owner of the savings bank is the Austrian financial group Erste Group Bank, which is a key player in the banking market in Central and Eastern Europe.
Vojtěch on Monday will unveil a system of three emergency levels against coronavirus
"Grades, according to the strength of the community spread at the level of the regions.
We are working on it together with epidemiologists and the Institute of Health Information and Statistics," Vojtěch told Czech Television.
It will be a so-called traffic light, individual regions will be marked with green, orange or red according to the risk of coronavirus.
The minister added that he will still discuss the inclusion of individual counties with epidemiologists.
He admitted that only the Moravian-Silesian region would have an orange color at the moment and Prague would be approaching it.
Individual measures for regional hygiene stations, hospitals, sanitary facilities and employers will also appear on the map.
"We want to deal with it at the level of the regions, because the situation in them is not uniform," Vojtěch said.
Waterman and Caroline
When the waterman Jonah and the miller's daughter Caroline fall in love, they don't have it easy at all.
Jonah is defended in love by his father, the grumpy waterman Hastrbuch, Karolínka has no idea that her dear Jonah is not human.
It's a good thing there's Libor, who's going to do everything he can to protect the whole village, and especially Karolínka, from that vodicky villain.
What about intriguing and lying?
Especially if she proves that Karolínka reject Jonah and give preference to him, Brave Libor.
Before long, it is clear to everyone in the village who is responsible for drowninging the successful Konvička, rotten flour, the fire of the mill and other villains.
Angry villagers will organize a hunt for a waterman, and an irreconcilable vodic curse will fall on Jonah's head.
And now Konvička's south, counsel Caroline!
How to soften the old Hastrbuch, convince the neighbors of Jonah's innocence, find Jonah and rescue him from the curse?
The fairy tale was filmed in September 2010 in ČT studios and in exteriors at the Brzina pond, in Obděnice in the Sedlčany region and in the beautiful environment of the historical mill in Hoslovice.
Mostecky autodrome launches new e-shop
Customers of the Mostek autodrome can use a new e-shop on its website.
It will also buy coupons for free and test car rides or a motorcycle on a large racing circuit, which was previously possible only in person at the autodrome worker in the starting tower.
"We've approached improving the e-shop so that it's user-friendly, and at the same time we've expanded the portfolio of products and services that can be purchased.
At the same time, on this occasion, we have prepared for our customers a bonus in the form of a discount of 1 000 crowns for all safe driving courses from our offer.
The discount coupon can be found at www.autodrom-most.cz," Jana Svobodová, sales and marketing director of AUTODROM MOST, explained.
According to her, the website of the e-shop is now clear, the customer is easy to navigate.
"An e-shop attendee chooses the focus he's targeting.
For example, sports, safe or sports riding courses, training, experiential programs, gift items, gift vouchers, activities for children and others," the director said.
Among the innovations in the products and services of the e-shop offered are, for example, the T-shirt of the Racing Legends collection with a surprise, a car track, a course of safe driving in reduced visibility, advertising and gift items of the domestic professional racing team Buggyra Racing, or Engelmuller gloves from the limited edition of the watchmaking manufacture BOHEMATIC, created on the occasion of a historic victory in the prestigious race of 24 Hours of Le Mans in 1949.
"The offer is very varied, the customer can choose from more than 200 items," Jana Svobodová said.
When paying for the selected product or service, the customer can choose one of four options.
Prepayment (bank transfer), cash on delivery, cash upon personal collection or online through the payment gateway.
The course of the disease of the majority is mild, only three percent of those infected have to go to the hospital
Either a milder form of coronavirus is spreading through the Czech Republic, or the summer dose of sunshine has significantly improved our immunity.
Infectious wards of hospitals are half-empty, even as infection numbers rise.
"The situation today and in April is completely different, and it is mainly due to the number of hospitalizations," confirms the Minister of Health Adam Vojtěch.
It is worth remembering that on April 11, when the first wave of the epidemic peaked in our country, everyone wore masks, collections were carried out by the army, the republic was closed and the Czechs consumed disinfection for a year in advance.
However, ten people died every day.
Now the daily increases in the number of infected people are higher, but every day with covid-19, a maximum of two people die.
Karvinsko still has the highest number of infected people per population. Jihlava already has similar numbers due to an outbreak in the local Alzheimer center, where there are already 57 infected - 40 clients and 17 employees.
Another home for the elderly was closed by hygienicians in Černožice in the Hradec Králové region.
The disease was confirmed by tests by one of the nurses.
"We're tracing, we've restricted movement, we've got all the hygiene measures," says Martin Schnal, the director of the retirement home.
According to him, none of the home's clients have symptoms of the disease yet.
Frequency testing will be carried out by hygienicians tomorrow and then decide on the next measure.
Hospitals are also respondinging to an increased number of those infected.
Today, the Institute of Oncology in Brno launched an automatic camera system at the entrance, which recognizes whether people are wearing a veil at the entrance.
The record is reported by South Bohemian health officials, who recorded twenty-five cases of coronavirus - most of them are made up of foreign employees of a dusty company.
In total, there were 131 people infected on Saturday - that is, half of Friday's numbers - but fewer are being tested over the weekend.
Debutant champion!
Van den Bergh finishes fairy tale, sweeped by Anderson
The great surprise of a tournament called Dimitri van den Bergh showed an excellent performance even in a duel with a player who won the second most prestigious event within the PDC in 2018.
The start of the match was mutually balanced, the break came in a state of 8:7 for the Belgian dartsman.
Van den Bergh took a break with a 13-darp leg and has had the upper hand ever since.
He literally crushed his opponent with his game and a significantly more experienced "Flying Scot" was hard to get to doubles.
It is also evidenced by the fact that Anderson played his first maximum only in the 24th leg.
Moreover, the young Belgian handled great high checkouts.
The last time he lost in the 11th leg, in an unfavorable state he "caught a big fish" and closed 170.
At the end of the match, there was tremendous frustration on Anderson.
Rather, he was just throwing away, and his behind the triumph-wielding opponent behaved absolutely sovereignly.
Van den Bergh transformed the first of three match darts and lived up to his name - he wrote his big dream.
Profiting a trophy named after Philo Taylor is all the more valuable for a Belgian dartsman because it's his first-rounded major.
The last player who was able to beat van den Bergh is Karel Sedláček.
In Nantes, charges have been filed over a cathedral fire, the man has confessed
The Cathedral of St. Peter and Paul in the heart of Nantes caught fire last saturday, and the fire severely damaged 16th-century stained glass windows and destroyed the organ.
Suspicions of arson were investigated by investigators due to the fact that the blaze progressed through the temple from three different locations.
At the same time, police found no signs at the entrances that anyone got into the cathedral by force.
The accused is a volunteer at a local diocese, a thirty-nine-year-old Rwandan refugee.
Police arrested him shortly after the fire, but then released him without prosecuting him.
Now prosecutors have reported that, based on new evidence, the man was arrested again on Saturday, pleading guilty and facing arson charges.
In the event that he is found guilty, he faces up to ten years in prison and a hefty fine.
According to his attorney, the man regrets the act and decided to fully cooperate with investigators, Le Monde wrote.
Vojtěch: On Monday we will present a "semaphore" for epidemiological measures
The new system is intended to fulfill the role of the pandemic plan, due to the absence of which the government and the Ministry of Health are facing criticism.
"The levels will be according to the strength of the community spread," the minister explained.
He added that, for example, the Moravian-Silesian Region or Prague would currently reach a medium, orange stage.
The system is designed as regional, because the situation in individual regions differs significantly, he added.
The Ministry cooperated with epidemiologists and the Institute of Health Information and Statistics (ÚZIS).
According to the minister, specific measures will be attached to the individual stages, whether it is the obligation to wear masks or the organization of special departments in hospitals.
To create an epidemiological plan on Prima Partie TV, Interior Minister Jan Hamáček (ČSSD) called, according to which people should have clear information about what measures they can expect if the epidemiological situation deteriorates.
The daily increase in confirmed cases in the Czech Republic has increased in recent days, although the number of hospitalizations remains low.
Vojtěch commented on the fact that there is no big deal to talk about declaring a state of emergency or convening a crisis staff.
Due to the increasing number of people infected across the Czech Republic since Saturday, the government has again introduced the obligation to wear masks at indoor events of more than 100 people.
From Monday, the capacity of these events will be limited to 500 people, now it amounts to 1,000 people.
The government's procedure, when it was not clear until the last moment what measures and when they would start to take effect or when they would be announced, received criticism.
"I think that in a situation that is quite tense, the main officials should, on the contrary, create a sense of security and, on the contrary, straighten out the confusion," Senator Marek Hilšer said in Questions.
The measures may already vary according to the region, for example, in Prague from Sunday there is an obligation to wear masks even in non-residential medical facilities, for example in pharmacies or in waiting rooms with a doctor.
The obligation to wear masks when visiting medical and social facilities or pharmacies has also been introduced since Friday in the Liberec and Usti Regions.
It is necessary to wear masks in indoor public areas, public transport and medical facilities from 14 July.
On the contrary, anti-epidemic measures in the Moravian-Silesian Region were relaxed, as well as in the Central Bohemian Region in the Kutná Hora region.
At the beginning of June, the Czech government also approved a so-called traffic light for Europe, which distinguishes the level of risk of infection in individual European countries, and accordingly regulates the rules applicable to entry into the Czech Republic from these countries.
In low and medium-risk countries since June 15, the authorities do not require Czech citizens to test for coronavirus or order quarantine instead.
They are mandatory for the return of Czechs from countries with a high risk.
At the moment, all EU member states are now in a category with a low risk of contagion.
Technoparty in the Benešov region continues.
She's playing for one-third capacity, she was supposed to finish at noon
Hlaváčkova Lhota (Benešovsko) Technoparte near Hlaváčkova Lhota in the Benešov region continues on Sunday evening.
A third of the original 12 music systems are still playing.
According to evening estimates of the police, about 500 people remain here after 20:00, the original rough estimates talked about three hundred, in the morning there were 900, police spokeswoman Lucie Nováková said.
Originally, the technoparty was supposed to end at noon on Sunday, at that time a part of the music systems actually ceased.
"Some have already left, and others are about to leave.
Musical produces are still partially ongoing.
We expect that there will be a gradual disconnection and dismantling of musical systems," Nováková said.
"Officers are still consistently inspecting departing visitors in order to prevent drivers from driving under the influence of alcohol or addictive substances.
Drivers have the opportunity to undergo a precautional breath test before leaving," she added.
Dozens of riot police officers supervise the event, there are foreign police, traffic and riot police on the scene, and there are also dog handlers with service dogs on alert.
"During today, police officers inspected 470 people moving at the venue of the event and 485 vehicles, 270 drivers were subjected to a breath test, of which six sat behind the wheel under the influence of alcohol," Nováková said.
A total of 95 drivers were tested by police for the presence of narcotic and psychotropic substances, of which one was positive.
Officers also handled 14 traffic violations.
At the request of the participants of the technoparty, the officers carried out 300 preventive breathing tests and four preventive tests for the presence of narcotic drugs and psychotropic substances.
There were about 2000 people at the event on Saturday morning.
If the number of participants had not been reduced, police would have intervened there.
Interior Minister Jan Hamacek (ČSSD) said this on the Prima Partie television programme.
However, the number of participants decreased below 1,000, so the police evaluated that the intervention is not necessary, nor did the hygiene station or the Ministry of Health ask for it.
Czech Television (ČT) said on Sunday that the techno-party had also spread to the surrounding land, whose owners did not agree with the use for techno parties.
It is possible that the owners of the surrounding land will seek damages.
Supporters of techno in the meadow near Hlaváčkova Lhota arrived on Friday evening.
On Friday, the police guarded a meadow in Blažim in the Benešov region, where the techno-party took place last year.
Blažim is located about 30 kilometers from Hlaváčkova Lhota, where the technicians finally really gathered.
According to Novakova, no one came to Blažim.
Viper bite: The little girl ended up in the hospital!
"This year's first case was recorded in Železnorudsk.
The viper was bitten by a girl there, the year 2010, which the parents themselves brought to the local base of the emergency services," Mária Svobodová, spokeswoman for the emergency medical service, told the newspaper, adding that the child was transported to a hospital in Klatovy.
Viper is the only poisonous snake in the Czech Republic and it is in Šumava that these animals are found in higher numbers, so it is good to be careful during walks.
However, there is no great danger from these animals.
The chances of a viper biting you are not too high, they are afraid of themselves in normal contact.
And if she's comfortable with it, she'd be happy to crawl away with a loud hiss.
They only attack if they feel very endangered - for example, when you accidentally step on it.
However, its bite is not a tragedy, although its poison is very effective against small mammals, but it does not kill people.
This can only happen if a person has a particularly weakened organism or suffers from allergies.
Therefore, the risk of danger is mainly in children, seniors and allergy sufferers.
"A person often feels sick, sweats, has an elevated temperature, thirst, abdominal pain and diarrhea, in weakened individuals and especially allergy sufferers can experience anaphylactic shock," Miriam Gredová from Klatovská Hospital told the newspaper.
The man threatened to throw the infant out the window.
He poured wine and threw dishes at the policewoman
After 23:00, an intervention unit entered the apartment and detained the man.
The infants were immediately taken into care by paramedics, regional police spokeswoman Markéta Janovská said in a press release.
The man did not want to cooperate with the officers, after a police negotiator threw dishes, knives or poured wine from the window.
He amplified his threats several times during the negotiations by leaning from the window with the child in his arms.
Once he even climbed onto the windowsill where he staggered.
Police are continuing to investigate the man.
He doesn't want to publish more information yet.
Stosurova will give preference to her daughter over the tennis season
Australian tennis player Samantha Stosur will no longer be involved in the coronavirus-affected season this year.
Thirty-six-year-old U.S. Open champion 2011, who became a mother in June but doesn't want to end her career yet.
He plans to return to the courts next year and believes that thanks to his family and daughter, he will enjoy tennis even more than before.
Stosur announced on social media two weeks ago that her partner Liz Astling gave birth to daughter Genevieve in mid-June.
“I’m going to stay at home, I’m going to spend time with my family, I’ll be with all the little milestones our Evie will experience in the next six months and I’m just going to enjoy,” she said.
The WTA Tour will resume from March's interrupted season on August 3, with a clay-court tournament in Palermo.
Sapa market: Raid revealed meat, fishes and eggs of unfamiliar origin
"One of the vehicles entering the premises transported 1320 pieces of quail eggs of unfamiliar origin, in addition, in inappropriate conditions.
Also, these eggs will be disposed of in the sanitation plant," the State Veterinary Administration said.
When inspecting the stand with live fish, it was then found that the seller did not have evidence of the origin of the goods and was not registered for the sale of live fish.
From a veterinary point of view, the market is considered problematic for a long time.
For example, last autumn, veterinarians uncovered an illegal grocery store, with almost half a ton of unmarked food undocumented of origin.
For most it was not possible to determine the date of consumption, part of the products had an expired shelf life and were spoiled.
For five pounds of sepia, the shelf life date was exceeded by three years.
Last May, veterinarians and police officers in the marketplace's premises detained 80 kilograms of food without proof of origin, including dried fish, squid or frozen ducks, part of which were moldy.
Putin was on the phone with Zelensky ahead of a ceasefire in Donbas, the Kremlin criticizes planned elections
Russian President Vladimir Putin spoke by phone with his Ukrainian counterpart Volodymyr Zelensky about the situation in Donbass, where a new ceasefire is scheduled to take effect from midnight.
According to the Kremlin statement, Putin criticized, among other things, the decision of the Ukrainian parliament to hold local elections; Kiev reported that Zelenskyy called for the release of detained Ukrainians.
Negotiators of Ukraine and Russia agreed on a negotiation on Wednesday evening with the support of the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe (OSCE).
The ceasefire is scheduled to begin after midnight at night on Monday, to be "complete and general" and to open the way for further provisions of peace agreements.
In an interview, Putin told Zelensky that the Ukrainian parliament's decision to hold local elections contradicts the Minsk peace agreement and threatens the prospects of settling the conflict in eastern Ukraine.
Last week, Ukraine's parliament decided that the upcoming elections to local councils would not take place on territories controlled by pro-Russian rebels.
The Minsk agreement foresees that Kiev will regain control of the border of Ukrainian countries occupied by separatists with Russia after providing these regions with broad autonomy and holding local elections here.
According to Unian, Zelensky’s office said the two statesmen agreed on the benefits of the upcoming ceasefire in Donbass as well as the need to implement additional measures to promote peace in the region, such as demining areas of combat or withdrawing part of troops from the front.
Zelenskyy also called on Putin for the release of Ukrainian detainees in eastern Ukraine, the occupied peninsula of Crimea and Russia itself.
The Minsk agreement helped stop the fighting, but in occasional gunfights and artillery attacks, people continue to die on both sides of the front.
The Conflict in the Donbass has claimed more than 14,000 deaths since the spring of 2014.
Ukraine and the West accuse Russia of military support for separatists, which Moscow denies.
Zelenskyy has made ending the war in Donbass his priority, but there has not yet been a major breakthrough in negotiations.
An unexpected champion in the decathlon is Rykl, the decathlon was dominated by Novotná
Defender of the last two titles Doležal did not finish at the end of Saturday's four-hundred program, Kopecký did not jump the foundation in the stick on Sunday.
The decathlon was long ruled by Latvian representative Reinis Kregers, who is preparing in coach Josef Karas's group, but resigned after the disk.
Rykl had two bronze medals from the outdoor MČR, this time he was the only decathloner who got over the seven-thousand mark.
Other medals were awarded to Vilém Stráský and Petr Urbánek.
Rykl recalled the absence of Adam Sebastian Helcelet and Jiří Sýkora after the competition.
"It's too bad there weren't guys here and Kope didn't start because the results would have looked very different.
But the title tastes very good, I especially appreciate the fact that I gave myself a person by 30 centimeters in the distance.
Then I was pleased with the stick and I was most disappointed with the disk, where I was below average," he acknowledged on Facebook.
Kateřina Cachová won with more than a hundred-point advantage on the first day, despite being troubled by an injured ankle on a rebounding leg.
However, in the long-distance sector she registered only 570 cm, did not swipe 40 meters and did not enter the final eight hundred.
Novotná collected 5878 points and won by 241 points ahead of Kateřina Dvořáková.
It was mainly distanced, 631 cm is the best Czech performance of the year.
“I really wanted it to be around 630.
I started pulling my calf yesterday, but I'm very glad that I didn't feel it at all and that it didn't limitate me at all," Novotná said on Facebook.
Bio LPG has arrived in the Czech Republic, promises ecological operation
Liquefied Petroleum Gas (LPG) is a well-known alternative fuel in the Czech Republic.
It is especially popular because of its fundamentally lower price compared to gasoline or diesel, due to a significantly lower excise tax, but its benefit is even in lower CO2 emissions per kilometer.
Like gasoline and diesel, it also has its eco-friendly variant - "bio LPG".
However, it is quite new - it first appeared on the European market last year and arrived in the Czech Republic a few days ago, according to a press report by the Czech LPG Association.
"In these days we have received the first fifteen tons of bio LPG, which will be distributed in a mixture with classical fossil LPG to our gas stations," the association quotes Jiří Karlík from Primagas as saying.
However, organic LPG cannot be taken as bioethanol or biodiesel.
According to the association, it is chemically identical to the classic oil LPG, which a number of motorists now refuel into their cars.
Therefore, it is also possible to mix both versions freely.
"The only difference compared to classical LPG is the method of production," Ivan Indráček, chairman of the association, says of bio LPG.
This is one of the great advantages of organic LPG compared to other alternative fuels.
It can be easily transported and stored, and also the distribution infrastructure is relatively good, e.g. in comparison with hydrogen or electricity.
Currently, this gas is obtained as a by-product in the production of bioetanol from food residues and sugar cane.
In the future, it should also be produced specifically, e.g. from cellulose biomass, i.e. from wooden waste.
On the other hand, classical petroleum LPG arises partly as a by-product of oil refining, partly by separation in the extraction of natural gas.
As a result, depending on the production technology and the source, the operation of the car on bio LPG is 40-70% more environmentally friendly in terms of carbon footprint than operating on petroleum LPG and up to 90% compared to diesel.
But it is not about CO2 emissions from the exhaust of the car, but about the ecology of fuel production, i.e. the so-called part "well-to-tank".
One of the largest producers of organic LPG currently includes the petrochemical companies Eni, Total and Neste.
According to a recent update of the National Clean Mobility Action Plan (NAP ČM), Unipetrol is also exploring the possibility of production in its Litvínov refinery.
According to estimates by the association in the Czech Republic, about 170 thousand vehicles run on LPG; the Ministry of the Environment lists a slightly higher number in the NAP of ČM, about 200 thousand.
According to statistics from the Ministry of Transport, almost 6,000 LPG-powered cars were carried out last year alone.
It is possible to refuel LPG in the Czech Republic at about 950 stations of different operators.
VojtĚch reviewed the number of infected, the number dropped by 1775 cases
Minister of Health Adam Vojtěch (for ANO) has ordered the ministry to revise the number of people infected with the coronavirus virus.
New statistics were released by the Department of Health on its website on Sunday.
The number on Sunday afternoon dropped from Saturday's 5254 to 3479 cases, which is 1775 fewer infected.
There are currently 3422 patients in the Czech Republic.
"Behind the jump in the number of cured is the revision of the data, which has not recently been updated by hygienicians," said Jaroslav Dušek, the head of health statistics.
By Sunday afternoon, 15 212 people were infected with coronavirus in the Czech Republic, 11 419 have recovered and 371 Covid-19 diseases have unfortunately succumbed.
Mobile electoral units or correspondence elections.
Politicians and sociologists were looking for variants for the time of the pandemic
According to Deputy Prime Minister and Interior Minister Jan Hamáček (ČSSD), a change in the law is necessary in order to allow people to vote in the regional and Senate elections due to the coronavirus.
It could be debated by parliament in August or September in an accelerated regime.
He stated this on Sunday on Partia TV Prima.
On Tuesday, he wants to discuss possible solutions with representatives of parliamentary parties at the ministry.
"Whatever we do, it will have to mean a change in the electoral law," Hamas said.
On Wednesday, the Senate urged the government to find a solution under current statutory rules.
However, the minister said it would be necessary to adjust the provisions of the law, according to which people in quarantine due to a medical condition cannot vote.
For example for patients with open tuberculosis should be maintained.
Daniel Prokop said in the OVM show that the possibility of correspondence has been postponed since 2004.
Even the current bill, which is in the Chamber of Deputies, then envisages its introduction for voting abroad, but not in the Czech Republic.
At the same time, Prokop considers the concerns of ANO or ČSSD (because, according to experience from abroad, it would rather record other types of parties) from the correspondence's choice to be overvalued.
Personally, it is then against the possibility of electronic voting, where there is a considerable possibility of abuse.
Michal Klíma said that it is now necessary ad hoc to devise mechanisms (elections) that could be carried out already this year and with the least possible health risk.
He considers it to be prepared for 2021, when parliamentary elections are supposed to take place in the Czech Republic.
He considers correspondence (whether only abroad or also in the Czech Republic) to be one of the functional methods.
As the next he sees the above-mentioned electoral units or, for example, the possibility of a proxy
He added that there is a chance to learn how similar situations -⁠ elections during the coronavirus -⁠ were dealt with in Croatia, Poland or France.
Due to the spread of the coronavirus outbreak, different types of elections have already been postponed in 67 countries.
Protests in Buharska: Pig's ears threw protesters at government building
Participants of the demonstration against Bulgarian Prime Minister Boyka Borisov and his cabinet threw pig's ears at the government building.
It was another of a series of protests against the "mafia style of governance" that has been taking place in Sofia and other cities for nearly three weeks.
Organizers are planning a nationwide blocade for Wednesday, the DPA news agency said.
Today's protest, which took place on the 18th consecutive day, had a weaker turnout, according to Radio Free Europe/Radio Svoboda.
By 20:00, about 300 people gathered, some streets had to be enclosed for traffic.
The protesters in front of the building of the government played down a theatrical scene in which they symbolically confined both Prime Minister Borisov and Attorney General Ivan Geshev in connection with the arrest of two advisers to President Rumeno Radev, who is a strong political opponent of the Prime Minister.
Resignation of both is one of the basic demands of protesters.
They also demanded the abolition of the announced health care reform and the end of corruption in construction on the Black Sea coast.
Protest organizer Nikolai Hajigenov said in public radio today that a "peak of escalation" in the form of a "nationwide demonstration in Sofia and blockades across the country" is planned on Wednesday.
He reiterated the demand for the resignation of Borisov and his government, as well as calling early elections.
The proper ones are expected to take place in March 2021.
Borisov has previously said he does not intend to resign.
He justified, among other things, the need to implement Bulgaria through the covid-19 pandemic and the resulting economic crisis.
On Friday, however, he replaced five members of his governmental team, even though he did not calm the protesters.
Earlier this week Borisov's government overcame a vote of confidence in parliament in parliament.
Coach Boloni Mihajlovich doesn't want matches without spectators anymore
Boloni Sinisha Mihajlovich, a football coach, already has had enough of Italian league matches without spectators.
The Serbian coach hopes that from the new season fans will return to the stadiums.
"What we're doing here at the moment isn't football.
I'm tired of matches without spectators.
It's sexless football, it doesn't bring me any emotion, no joy.
Those matches are worse than training.
Playing like this is terrible and I don't think I'm the only one who thinks so," Mihajlovic said before today's game against Lecce.
After the coronavirus hiatus, Series A's struggles are in quick succession, and even though they don't play until the evening, their actors are exposed to heat.
"We play every three days in terrible heat.
In such conditions, it doesn't even know how the teams are.
For this season to be over.
Those measures had to be approached in order to finish the competition, but I hope that from September the fans will be back," said the coach, who has also led Fiorentina or AC Milan in the past.
For Mihajlovič, this season is also extraordinary because four months on the bench was missing due to leukemia treatment.
"Despite my illness, the interruption of the league and everything else, our team managed to move up the level.
We want to continue to grow and for that we need the players to be mentally stronger," he said.
We've become the target of a cyberattack of foreign states, Iran says
Since the end of June, fires and explosions have been reported from a number of Iranian military, industrial and nuclear installations, as well as oil refineries.
Some Iranian officials said the fire, which erupted this month in underground nuclear operations in Natanzu, may have caused cyber sabotage.
The cause of a number of other incidents has not yet been explained.
Iranian Foreign Ministry spokesman Abbas Musavi told reporters, according to Iranian media, that the Islamic Republic's infrastructure faces "thousands of cyberattacks" on a daily basis.
He added that it was not a new phenomenon and that most of them would be repelled.
In recent months there have been several broader cyberattacks, according to Musawi, behind which, according to analyses, "certain governments and groups" stood.
However, he did not give further details about the possible perpetrators, according to Reuters.
Musavi also added that fires in forests, refineries and other places are frequent in the summer.
An article by the Iranian state-run IRNA agency this month discussed that enemies of Iran, like Israel and the U.S., may have been behind recent incidents.
However, none of these countries have been directly charged with specific attacks in the article.
Iran and the United States severed diplomatic ties shortly after the 1979 revolution when Iranian militant students attacked the U.S. Embassy in Tehran and held 52 Americans hostage for 444 days.
Long-terrified relations between the two countries escalated in 2018 after Washington withdrew from the 2015 nuclear deal between Iran and world powers and imposed sanctions on Iran that have a harsh impact on its economy.
Another significant escalation came this year after the U.S. military on January 3 killed Iranian General Qasem Soleimani and a member of Iraque militias Abu Mahdi Muhandis.
On January 8, Iran fired a number of missiles at bases in Iraq where the Americans operate.
The U.S. admitted that dozens of U.S. soldiers had been wounded.
Israel considers Iran its arch-enemy, and Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu is a major opponent of Iran's 2015 international nuclear deal.
According to the Israeli prime minister, Iran is trying to develop an atomic bomb, which Iran denies.
TPCA has extended production shutdown until May 25
Cologne-based automaker Toyota Peugeot Citroën Automobile (TPCA) has re-extended production shutdown due to the coronavirus.
Production lines are expected to relaunch on Monday, May 25 in the morning.
Dozens of hygiene measures will accompany the run-in, Tomáš Paroubek, a spokesman for the automaker, said.
Production has been standing due to the coronavirus pandemic since the evening of March 18.
Originally, the company intended to restore it on Monday, May 18.
The reason for extending the shutdown, according to Paroubek, are closed borders and closed dealerships, so there is no one to sell cars.
According to Paroubek, the company will produce 100 percent from the beginning, i.e. for two shifts.
Coronavirus is affected by the firm's key markets, such as Italy, the UK or Spain.
Mayor Michael Kašpar (Change for Cologne) said the production shutdown at the TPCA did not sign off on life in the city.
"At first it was obvious, it was empty in the city.
But gradually the streets are filling up, especially now since Monday, when it's eased a lot.
So the city is full," Kašpar said.
According to him, there are no complications even with foreign workers who have stayed at hostels.
"Over those two months, we didn't have to deal with almost any problem," Kaspar said.
For example, Mladá Boleslav-based Škoda Auto, which also discontinued production due to the coronavirus crisis, resumed it on Monday, April 27, at all three of its production plants in the Czech Republic.
Neither of the races operates at 100 percent capacity.
More than 80 hygiene measures accompanied the resumption of production in Škoda.
During the night from Monday to Tuesday in Mladá Boleslav and Kvasiny, for the first time after the resumption of production, a third night shift took place.
Approximately 2500 people currently work at TPCA and the company represents one of the largest exporters in the Czech Republic and also an employer in Cologne.
Cancer killed bride shortly after wedding: She left a note to family
For more than two years, Gemma Sisson had been battling an insidious cancer, and when she appeared to have beaten the cancer, the disease came again.
The thirty-nine-year-old woman, who loved bodybuilding, eventually ended up on a bed in a hospice, where she was still able to marry the love of her life.
Unfortunately, she succumbed to the disease.
A few hours after her death, a shocking message for those closest to him appeared on her Facebook account.
Gemma Sisson, from Leeds, England, loved bodybuilding.
With boyfriend Ricky Moore, with whom she's lived for more than 16 years, they have traveled to competitions taking place around the world.
A thirty-nine-year-old woman played sports and lived healthily, yet she was diagnosed with cancer in April 2018.
Gemma has begun exhaustive treatment, radiotherapy, in the hope of getting better.
After months of treatment, Gemma actually appeared to have managed to win over cancer, but in August 2019 the cancer returned and this time attacked the spine.
According to the Daily Mirror, the bodybuilder and personal trainer underwent ten-hour spinal surgery and then began drastic treatment again.
But everything was in vain.
Earlier this year, on her thirty-nineth birthday, doctors told her she had a few months to live.
In April, the handsome blonde finally decided to join a hospice in Leeds, where she was visited by her family every now and then, who even arranged a wedding with her love Ricky.
The marriage lasted two months.
Ricky took Gemma to the restaurant for the last time for their two-month anniversary.
Four days later, Gemma died in his arms.
Just a few hours after her death, a post appeared on her social media account that Gema had written for her family.
"If this has been published, it means that I have finally died after weeks of fighting too long.
Although I'll be heartbroken that I'm leaving my loved ones here, I'll be so glad if I get rid of what this terrible disease has caused me.
“I can’t thank Ricky enough, who has been by my side all the time and has given me a huge amount of love and support during that terrible time,” she wrote on social media, adding that the pain and suffering have finally ceased and she has found peace.
"I'm also grateful for all the support I've received from all of my followers on social media," she wrote, noting that everyone should value their life and live it to the fullest.
Art vending machines sell sweat dating, mushroom amulet and pexeso
