Salaries in the health sector will rise by a maximum of five percent next year.
According to Vojtěch, a bigger increase is unrealistic
Salaries in health care could rise by a maximum of five percent next year, Health Minister Adam Vojtěch (for ANO) told Czech Television.
The trade union's demand for an increase of up to 15 per cent is considered unrealistic.
He also said the state should try to be self-sufficient in securing protective equipment.
According to him, the hospitals now have their supplies.
"I think the increase (salaries) may be somewhere up to five percent," the minister said.
According to him, it is necessary to reflect on the current situation, when since March intervened in the selection of the coronavirus insurance pandemic and government regulations against its spread.
Income of insurance companies declined at that time.
"By 15 percent I find the increase unrealistic at the moment," Vojtěch said.
The average monthly earnings of a doctor in acute inpatient care according to the data of the Institute of Health Information and Statistics (ÚZIS) will probably reach 88 312 crowns this year, nurses 47 498 crowns.
The difference between the salary of nurses in state hospitals and wages for other founders will be up to seven thousand crowns.
The ÚZIS data also shows that the decline of nurses in hospitals has stalled.
The average wage in the Czech Republic in 2019, according to data from the Czech Statistical Office, was 34 125 crowns.
Representatives of trade unionists said in June that they would demand a minimum increase of ten percent for 2021, ideally by 15 percent.
Criticize in the long-term significant difference between the earnings of doctors and nurses in state hospitals, which must be governed by salary tables, and contractual wages in hospitals of cities, counties or private.
Therefore, they also demand that pay tables be uniform for health professionals.
For nurses this year, the difference will be up to seven thousand crowns a month.
For example, for doctors, wages grew faster than salaries last year.
According to the president of the Czech Dentistry Chamber, Roman Šmucler, trade unionists should perceive how it is currently.
Due to the effects of the Covid epidemic, the House recently approved an increase in this year's state budget deficit to a record 500 billion crowns, of which 1.75 billion are to go to the rewards of health workers in hospitals and social services.
According to Šmucler, the council of health care providers disagrees with the across-the-board increase in salaries next year.
According to him, hospitals and medical facilities themselves should decide on the remuneration, and the extra money should go to them through the reimbursement decree.
VojtĚch also stated that hospitals currently have enough protective equipment.
Pavel Švagr, Chairman of the Administration of State Material Reserves (SSHR), said this week that the administration does not currently own a single protective medical aid.
He's awaiting the completion of the selection procedures.
According to Vojtěch, suppliers in the world are now able to deliver goods better than in March, when they were redeemed.
According to him, the state should also try to be as self-sufficient as possible and buy from Czech companies.
Swinging clever quarantine could be taken over by soldiers.
We're ready, they're saying
