According to him, the army is ready for this step.
The smart quarantine project was taken over by the Department of Health on May 25.
In the meantime, the clever quarantine was led by a ninety-member central management team established under the army as an advisory body of the government, led by the former minister's deputy Roman Prymula.
The screeching project was criticized by Interior Minister Jan Hamáček (ČSSD) on Sunday.
He, too, has decreed that the army could take hold of him again.
The government envoy for IT and digitization Vladimír Dzurilla, who is in charge of him, has previously acknowledged weaknesses in the project.
"The system works, but it's still being worked on.
Twenty years were created independently and unsystematically, now we are trying to unify it in a few months.
It can't blow.
People on the move, the poor in the hygiene on the march adapt those new systems and actually catch the mistakes," he said.
Ostrava mayor Macura will not run again, because he doesn't want to "fool around in politics"
The current mayor of Ostrava, Tomáš Macura (ANO), will not run in the next local elections.
The reason for this is an effort to "don't fool around in politics," he told news server Respekt.cz.
Macura, who is also a member of the presidency of the ANO movement, has been the mayor of Ostrava since November 2014.
In municipal elections in 2018, the leader of the ANO movement in Ostrava won almost a third of the vote.
Macura originally wanted to be in office only for four years.
"I've told myself that I'm going to last one term, and with the business experience, I've been able to turn everything over here in that time and put it on my feet," he told Respect.
He added that he had allowed himself to be persuaded to some extent for a second term.
"But for me it's the maximum.
I don't want to fool around in politics," added Macura, who in an interview described himself as a tough introvert who doesn't like frequent contact with people.
According to Respekt, there has been speculation for a long time about Macura's possible end in the ANO movement.
The server recalls that Macura has repeatedly criticized the ANO movement, for example for its alliance with the SPD movement or for supporting Miloš Zeman in the presidential election.
As one of the few ANO personalities, he supported Zeman’s opposing candidate Jiří Drahoš in the elections.
Macura does not plan to leave the ANO movement he joined in 2017.
"Until I feel that I can change anything in the movement, that it's not a completely vain fight and that no one's involved, I don't have an imminent motive to end," he told Respect.
Sixty-six-year-old Macura graduated from the University of Economics in Prague.
Before he became mayor of Ostrava in 2014, Macura served as, among other other things, the director of the pharmaceutical company Walmark.
He also sat in the authorities of other companies.
Uber will return to Brno, offering transportation services
At the end of July, Uber, which offers transportation services, will return to Brno.
It is a consequence of the change in the law, Petr Kratochvíl (ODS), the councillor of Brno for transport, said in a press statement on Thursday.
Taxi drivers protested against Uber in Brno, pointing out that Uber does not meet conditions such as taxi markings or a taxi meter.
However, the change in the law has changed the conditions for the operation of ordinary taxis and alternative ones since July.
Kratochvíl has held several meetings in recent weeks with representatives of Uber.
"On this basis, the company decided at the end of July this year to return with its services to Brno.
This step is the result of a change in the law that allows this company to operate legally.
The city has been acquainted with this change and we believe that the company will operate its services within the given limits," Kratochvíl said.
Uber has worked in Brno in the past.
Taxi drivers protested against him because of unmarked vehicles as a taxi or a missing taximeter.
The court issued a preliminary injunction banning Uber from riding in the city.
Brno originally supported taxi drivers, but at the end of the year mayor Markéta Vaňková (ODS) said that the city would like to take steps for Uber to operate in Brno.
According to later decisions of the courts, Uber could ride in Brno, but the company did not take advantage of it.
It will now return on the basis of a change in the law.
From July, in addition to classic taxis with a flashlight and taximeter, it is easier to operate a transport service ordered and paid exclusively through mobile applications.
Too exuberant celebration in Brno: the flying chair landed a meter from a man on the pavement
An office chair flew from the fourth floor of the apartment on Saturday evening in Brno and landed about a meter from the pavement.
At that moment he was followed by a pedestrian, who immediately called the officers.
Immediately after arriving at the given address in the western part of the city, the patrol noticed an open window, under which there was still a chair, and next to it a few cans and butts.
"The noise, carried from the room on the fourth floor, suggested that a rather noisy celebration was taking place inside.
Officers went up the corridor to the apartment and asked three young men from twenty-six to twenty-nine for an explanation," police spokesman Jakub Ghanem said.
Babiš and ČSSD will discuss Rafaj’s request to recall Rafaj
Prime Minister Andrej Babiš (ANO) plans to discuss the demand for the dismissal of the head of the antitropolic office (ÚOHS), Petr Rafaj, on Monday.
Non-profit organizations have called for Rafaj’s dismissal at the anti-corruption council of the government, as well as the Ústecký region.
The Social Democrats say an exchange at the head of office is appropriate.
According to CTK, Rafaj was not invited to the Cabinet Office on Monday.
His second term expires in the middle of next year, according to the law he cannot be appointed for a third time.
His dismissal is within the competence of President Miloš Zeman.
In addition to the appeal of non-profit organizations, the prime minister also received a letter with a similar request from the Ústí region.
The reason, among other things, should be Rafaj's recent cases.
In the past week, the prime minister declined to comment on the request, saying he would familiarize himself with the materials and the government parties would take "some opinion" on it.
He also stated that he had not yet discussed the calls with the president, he does not have a meeting with him in the near future.
According to a statement from the Office on the appeal, there is no reason because Rafaj has not been charged with anything.
In the past week, IHNED.cz and Aktuálně.cz wrote that the police were interested in Rafaj’s meetings with Governor Jiří Čunek.
Among other things, the meetings were related to deciding on a competition for the project of a controversial new Zlín hospital for eight billion, which Čunek strongly promotes.
Čunek acknowledged the meetings, according to him, they jointly dealt with his "senate activities".
Rafaj speaks the same way.
The council of the Ústecký region called for the removal of Rafaj to the government at the end of June.
It also calls for a reform of the Office's decision-making mechanisms.
According to Jaroslav Komínek, Deputy Governor for Transport, the decision-making of the Office regarding public contracts is not transparent and has arbitraries.
Last March, police intervened at an antitrust office because of a toll-collecting tender.
In June, news server Aktuálně.cz pointed out that Rafaj is using apartments written on the company of influential behind-the-scenes ČSSD player Alexander Čuba.
However, a deposit of 3.5 million for an apartment in Brno was paid from Rafaj’s account.
The Chairman of the Office for the Protection of Competition is Rafaj since 9 July 2009, for a second term the president of the former Social Democrat MP appointed in June 2015.
In less than a year, Rafaj's second six-year term expires.
The Antitropolic Office Act states that the president can dismiss the chairman of the office on a proposal from the government.
It can only be revoked if it does not serve for more than six months, or seriously violates the dignity of its function or the independence and impartiality of the Office.
Employees collapsed with heat in the Blansko library.
Air conditioning will help
The new technology will cost 740 thousand and will be in operation in the second half of August.
“It’s good news for me.
With three children, we go to the library in the center of Blansko regularly.
However, it was virtually impossible to stay there during the warm months.
It's been really hot and there's bad air.
Ventilation didn't help.
That's why I'm glad for the air-conditioning," said Petr Novotný, one of Blansko's readers.
According to the library's director, Lukas Dlapa, temperatures in the building have often climbed up to over thirty degrees.
The southern wall of the building is glazed and on sunny days it acts as a greenhouse.
"In addition, thanks to the curvature of the building, the sun leans into it for much of the day.
At the same time, it is a big problem to ventilate individual departments, because overnight windows must be closed for safety reasons.
We often have thirty degrees in the library since this morning," Dlapa said.
The worst part was the study room and the reading room on the third floor.
And one floor up then the exhibition rooms and the principal's office.
There was also an unpleasant climate in the children's ward, which is located under the roof of the building.
“Several times it has happened that librarians collapsed at work and ended up on incapacity for work.
The problem also occurred regularly in the cultural room at lectures attended by dozens of people.
After a few minutes it was impossible to breathe there," Dlapa remarked.
Originally, representatives of the city wanted to do air conditioning in all areas of the library this year
In the end, there will be only three circuits out of five.
"Due to limited funding, air conditioning will have only the most problematic areas.
Study room and reading room on the third floor.
Furthermore, the cultural room and the children's wall-up," informed Jiří Svoboda of the Blanský Investment Department.
He added that in the first phase, the workers had already installed air-conditioning in these premises last autumn and modified the wiring.
Approximately for 360 thousand crowns.
The Blansko Municipal Library has almost three thousand registered readers.
Of those, about nine hundred are under the age of fifteen.
