Thousands protested in Israel against prime minister
Thousands of Israelis in several places in the country have protested against unemployment, corruption and the way Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu tackles the coronavirus crisis.
Manifestations took place, among other things, in front of Netanyahu's private villa in the city of Caesarea, near his official residence or in a park in Tel Aviv.
Protests have been held regularly in Jerusalem and Tel Aviv for several weeks.
Their participants are demanding Netanyahu's resignation because of the trial in which he is accused of corruption and fraud, and also over how the government is handling the next wave of the coronavirus outbreak.
"Bibi, go home," one of the protesters wrote on his banner, referring to Netanyahu's well-known nickname.
"Everyone sees that the emperor has lost his clothes," it said.
Seventy-year-old Netanyahu is Israel's first prime minister to serve.
He is accused in three cases of corruption, fraud and abuse of trust.
He denies all accusations and calls them a witch hunt.
According to local medias, the process can take up to three years, more than 300 witnesses are summoned.
The SARS-CoV-2 coronavirus has been confirmed in nearly 60,500 people in Israel, which has a population of about nine million.
455 people have died in connection with the outbreak.
In March, the Israeli government introduced quarantine measures to help reduce daily rises for those infected.
However, after the gradual easing of restrictions since the end of April, in particular, the number of infected people began to grow significantly again at the end of June.
Netanyahu's critics say the government hasn't adequately helped, for example, self-employed people affected by quarantine measures.
Protesters are also annoyed that the government is issuing contradictory instructions in places as part of the fight against coronavirus.
The driver smashed the car against a tree and died.
Firefighters had to remove the body from the wreckage
The tragic accident happened around 23 o'clock near Neveklov on a narrow district leading from the Slapy dam.
The driver apparently flew at considerable speed down into the woods and smashed the car against a tree.
He perished at the scene.
Firefighters had to extricate the body from the wreckage, among other things cutting off the roof.
Circumstances of the crash are being investigated by police officers.
They ordered a judicial autopsy to clarify, among other things, whether the man was driving under the influence of alcohol or any other addictive substance.
End with waste and spewing one collection after another.
Pietro Filipi cancels discounts and bets on sustainability
Czech clothing brand Pietro Filipi wants to focus on sustainability and embarks on a fundamental change of strategy.
He's canceling discounts and doesn't want to spew one collection after another anymore.
The fact that the fashion industry is one of the biggest polluters of the environment is nothing new.
Billions of garments end up in incinerators and landfires every year.
However, the view of slow fashion, often a despised approach to dressing, has changed quite quickly in recent years.
Approximately half of Pietro Filipi's goods were sold at full price.
The second half, which was sold thanks to discounts, will not be easily bought.
The company does not want to spew one collection after another and at the end of each season to make the products cheaper below their production price.
Moreover, according to the owner Michal Mička, the times of new and old collections are gone, followed by rapidly changing fashion trends.
"Most merchants calculate discounts already when buying goods, so a much higher margin is included in the full price.
So it's actually a bit of a scam," says Pietro Filipi owner Michal Mička.
In addition, with its approach, the company also wants to support local business.
"Each of our products is made of carefully selected quality fabrics and designed by local designers.
We emphasize that a large part of our collection consists of so-called basic pieces - i.e. clothing that is timeless and overseasonal.
Globally, the segment of premium brands is typical in the absence of any discounts, the price of the product simply corresponds to its quality and the discount is not needed," adds Mička.
Giant graffiti introduces RAF flyer Kurt Taussig
"The assignment was to create something for the anniversary of the end of the war.
And I remembered our fighter pilot, who fought in the ranks of Britain's Royal Air Force in World War II.
The city management liked the proposal," said Vacek, who completed his work after a fortnight of intensive work.
It consumed about 300 liters of paint and 50 sprays.
Kurt Taussig was born in 1923 in Teplice-Šanov.
He left town when he was 15.
During the war, because of his Jewish background, the Nazis dragged him to an internation camp, where they held him for several weeks.
However, thanks to Nicholas Winton's organization, him and his brother managed to avoid a concentration camp.
In 1939, Taussig arrived in Great Britain, where he voluntarily joined the Royal Air Force (RAF), and later underwent pilot training in African Southern Rhodesia and Palestine.
He became involved in combat operations in early 1945.
After the Velvet Revolution, he visited the Czech Republic and his native Teplice several times, granting him honorary citizenship.
Forty-six-year-old Kamil Vacek, who has been known to people in the street graphic art industry under the nickname Camo, has been working with sprays in Teplice streets for more than 20 years.
Graffiti started out on areas, which were earmarked for these purposes by then mayor Jaroslav Kubera (ODS).
Storm Hanna has reached the coast of Texas
Hurricane Hanna arrived on the southwest coast of the U.S. state of Texas on Saturday afternoon.
It hit the Texas island of Padre Island, roughly 24 kilometers north of Port Mansfield, and weakened to a tropical storm when moving above the mainland.
According to meteorologists, however, it still poses a great danger to residents of coastal areas.
Heavy winds and intense rainfall, along with local flooding, are reported with power outages and various material damages, including damaged ships, according to the Associated Press.
Texas Governor Greg Abbott had already declared a state of natural disaster for 32 counties in southwest Texas on Saturday, recommending voluntary evacuation.
The strongest warning was given to people from the coastal belt between Port Mansfield and Mesquite Bay, but in total it is supposed to hit an area almost 500 kilometers long before it gradually moves over northeastern Mexico.
"Although Hanna is now a tropical storm, heavy rain and wind, level rises, dangerous surf and unique tornadoes can pose a threat," the U.S. National Hurricane Center (NHC) warned.
This morning local time (afternoon CEST), the storm moved in the area of the U.S.-Mexican border and was accompanied by winds of 85 kilometers per hour.
Hanna became the first hurricane of this year's hurricane season in the Atlantic region on Saturday.
It was accompanied by winds of up to 145 kilometers per hour, but it gradually weakened to 112 kilometers per hour, which knocked Hanna out of the hurricane category again.
Authorities urged local residents to weather the storm at home without forgetting hygiene measures related to preventing the spread of coronavirus.
Texas is one of those U.S. states that the covid-19 pandemic plagues the most.
Due to the existing security measures and the need to comply with distances, possible evacuations are also a big challenge.
Provisional shelters were opened in hotels, schools and gyms.
In Hidalgo county of Texas, one of the buildings was also adapted as shelter after evacuees who tested positive for coronavirus or were otherwise exposed to contagion.
Local authorities urged all evacuees to take masks and disinfectant gels with them to shelter if possible.
"It's far from over yet.
We expect catastrophic flooding," Chris Birchfield, who works for the National Weather Service (NWS), was quoted as saying.
Predictions say that in some places it could rain up to 300 millimeters of precipitation in just a few hours, in some cases almost 500 millimeters, which, together with the rise of sea level, could flood a number of settlements on the coast.
Zeman lends the general's rank to Colonel Lipka
President Miloš Zeman will lend the general rank to Colonel Pavel Lipka on Wednesday.
This is according to an overview of the expected events of the Ministry of Defence.
The reason for lending the rank of brigadier general is Lipka's succession as deputy commander of the alliance's multinational division northeastern in Poland.
Lipka still works as the deputy commander of the ground forces of the Czech army.
Zeman's promotion at the end of June was proposed by the government.
Since the beginning of August, Lipka will replace the brigadier general Karel Řehka as the deputy commander of the multi-national division northeast in Elblago, Poland, which commands the units of the alliance's forward presence on the territory of Poland and the Baltics.
Řehka became director of the National Cyber and Information Security Authority (NÚKIB) in March.
The government has proposed to the president that Lipko be bestowed on the rank of brigadier general.
Pursuant to the Occupational Soldiers Act, a higher rank may be given to a soldier for the duration of his/her elected duties or for the duration of his/her duties, which requires a higher rank.
The position of deputy commander of the alliance division corresponds to the general rank.
The soldier to whom the rank was bestowed has the same rights and responsibilities as if he were appointed to that rank.
However, he is not entitled to a salary adjustment that corresponds to the rank.
Leasing the rank takes precedency of the reason for which it was granted.
"Colonel Lipka demonstrates high professional readiness and is a model for subordinates.
It significantly participates in fulfilling the tasks of the armed forces of the Czech Republic, including cooperation with foreign partners and ensuring the participation of the army in foreign operations," the Ministry of Defence said in June about the proposal for promotion.
Wednesday's promotion will take place at 12:30 at the Prague Castle, it will be attended by Defense Minister Lubomír Metnar or Chief of the General Staff Aleš Opata.
EU countries have coronavirus standards for aviation
European Union countries have agreed on common hygiene standards for air travel designed to help curb the spread of the novel coronavirus.
This was announced by German Transport Minister Andreas Scheuer.
Among the agreed standards is compliance with the distance or wearing masks on the plane and at the airport, but the middle seats on the aircraft will not have to remain vacant.
This was reported by Reuters.
"I am pleased that my colleagues have adopted the German proposal for Europe and that we have been able to agree on these unified standards," Scheuer said at the European Aviation Conference.
Germany's transport ministry then added that officials had reached an agreement and that it had yet to be formally approved by ministers.
Agreed measures include the requirement to cover their mouths and noses for passengers from the age of six, to comply with the recommended distance during security check-ins and check-in at the airport.
There must also be plenty of fresh air on aircraft and all information must be available in several languages.
Airlines will also be able to occupy middle seats.
The common rules agreed upon by the officials meet at least some of the requirements of the airlines.
Different standards caused confusion.
A temperature record fell on Arctic Svalbard, measuring almost 22 degrees there
"In Longyearbyen, a 41-year-old record has fallen," the Norwegian Meteorological Institute tweeted.
In the evening, the temperature there rose to 21.7 degrees Celsius, surpassing by 0.4 degrees the record from 1979, he added.
Roughly two-thousand Longyearbyen is the largest city in Svalbard, located about 1300 kilometers from the North Pole.
Svalbard heats up faster than almost any other place on Earth.
This highlights the dangers arising from the warming of other parts of the Arctic -⁠ from Alaska to Siberia, according to last year's report by the Norwegian Center for Climate Study.
According to him, the average temperature in Svalbard has risen by three to five degrees since the 1970s.
By 2100, it can rise even by ten degrees if greenhouse gas emissions into the atmosphere are not reduced.
Temperature growth in Svalbard can result in melting of ever-frozen soil, jeopardizing the stability of many buildings, roads or airports, and can cause more avalanches and landslides.
Climate warming also threatens Arctic fauna, such as polar bears and seals, that depend on the ocean's icy cover.
Britons who return from Spain await mandatory two-week quarantine
Britain's cabinet has also urged citizens to avoid all non-necessary trips to mainland Spain due to the risk of spreading the covid-19 disease.
Therefore, the Canary and Balearic Islands are not mentioned, however, even after returning from them, people have to go into isolation for two weeks.
The new rules come into effect at midnight.
“Protection of public health is an absolute priority.
We have taken this decision to prevent the possible spread of the virus in Britain," British diplomacy said in a statement.
"We have always made it clear that if necessary, we will immediately remove a country from the safe list," she said.
As the British newspaper Metro noted, the latest announcement will hit the travel plans of thousands of Britons.
Spain is one of a very popular holiday destination.
Among other things, Britons are concerned about whether they will be paid sick leave if they have to end up in isolation for 14 days after returning from the holidays.
For example, Neil Hunter from the town of Sittingbourne booked a ten-day holiday to Lanzarote, they were due to leave on Tuesday.
The trip has been booked since December.
"We were supposed to go the whole family, my wife Amanda and my daughter Bethany, but to be honest, I don't know what's going to happen after this announcement.
“I work as an engineer and I don’t think my employer will agree to be quarantined for two weeks when I return from vacation.”
In the context of Spain, there are concerns that the country is at the beginning of a second wave of the epidemic.
During the last two days, more than 900 newly infected people have been added there in 24 hours.
Authorities in some areas are already tightening coronavirus measures, such as Catalonia trying to curb all nightlife and prevent people from meeting in bulk.
The Czech Foreign Ministry has recently highlighted an increased risk of contagion in Catalonia, Aragonia, the Basque Country and the Madrid region.
Dukovany's electricity buy-in model will be discussed by the government
On Monday, the government will discuss a model of the purchase of electricity from the planned new unit of the Dukovany Nuclear Power Plant.
The bill foresees that if the feed-in price is higher than the price of power electricity on the market, the difference will be paid by all consumers through tariffs.
In the latter case, when the feed-in price will be lower than the price on the market, the impact on consumers will be the opposite.
Power electricity is an unregulated part of the final price of electricity for customers.
The Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Industry Karel Havlíček (for ANO) and the energy company CEZ have previously stated that the more likely option is that people will save thanks to the construction for electricity.
Analysts, on the other hand, point out that the long-term outlook for electricity prices in the markets is always uncertain, as developments have shown several times in the past.
The intention has previously been criticized by environmental organizations.
"The purchase price can be both below and above the market price of power electricity.
Thus, the final impact in the time of applicability of the rule can be both negative and positive.
The impact will break down between customers according to the size of the circuit breaker - with lower consumption or electricity consumption - with higher electricity consumption," the Ministry of Industry and Trade (MPO) said in the material documents.
Havlíček said in May that he expects that the wholesale price of electricity will rise and the people at the Dukovany construction site will make money.
Otherwise, there will be an impact in tens of crowns a year for the family, which, according to him, is far less than people pay annually in invoices for electricity to support so-called supported resources.
He has previously estimated that the buyout price could range between 50 to 60 euros (about 1320 to 1600 Kč) per megawatt-hour (MWh).
The submitted material does not contain any draft amount, it is to be included only in the purchase contract.
The price of electricity on the Prague Energy Exchange for 2021, to which traders are now the most supplied, is currently around 45 euros (about 1190 Kč) per MWh.
"With the difference in the purchase price and the market price of power electricity in the amount of ten euros, it could amount to an average household with a consumption of 3.3 MWh per year - it can correspond to a four-member household, standard equipped with electrical appliances including an electric hob and an oven, which, however, does not use electricity for heating and hot water - less than 100 Kč/year," the MPO said.
“We live in an extremely turbulent time.
The coronavirus pandemic has shown us how vulnerable the global economy is.
For this reason, I consider the long-term prediction of market prices to be an explicitly speculative matter," Tomáš Voltr, executive director of the Energy financial group, previously told the Czech Press Agency.
The proposed law foresees that the purchase would be carried out by the MIT, or by a legal entity authorised by it owned by the state at a predetermined price.
The contract between the investor and the state, guaranteeing the conditions for the purchase of electricity, would be concluded for at least 30 years, with a possible extension of ten years, even repeated, but not longer than the end of the operation of low-carbon production.
The low-carbon production design defines as a nuclear reactor-connected electricity plant with an installed capacity with a minimum electrical output of 100 megawatts (MW) after 2030.
Havlíček previously stated that the new Dukovany unit should have an output of 1200 MW and produce nine terawatt-hours of electricity per year, which would correspond to a tenth of the expected Czech consumption.
The unit is expected to be put into operation in 2036.
The model of financing the new Dukovany bloc was approved by the government on Monday.
The state should finance the facility interest-free during construction, two percent interest is expected during operation.
The opposition disagrees with the model of financing, it also dislikes the possibility that Dukovany would be built by Russian or Chinese companies.
The state should participate in the construction of 70 percent.
The rest should be paid by ČEZ, which will also cover all possible additional costs.
CEZ CEO Daniel Beneš told ČTK in May that the expected amount for the construction of the block at current prices is about six billion euros (approximately 162 billion crowns).
Treaties between the state and CEZ on the construction of a new Dukovany bloc will be signed on Tuesday.
The Ministry of Regional Development withdrew material on support for tourism in the media for two billion
According to which the Ministry of Regional Development (MMR) planned to spend two billion crowns in the media, on the Internet and billboards for advertising on holidays in the Czech Republic, the authority withdrew from the comment procedure.
Minister Klára Dostálová (for ANO) told news server SeznamZpravy.cz that the material is still being worked on.
Zdopravy.cz pointed out the document in eKlep (electronic library of the legislative procession) on Friday.
The ministry justified the plan with a slump in tourism due to coronavirus.
During the coronavirus crisis, even the media recorded a slump in revenue.
The proposal drew criticism from both the opposition and tourism representatives.
According to the proposal, which can now be viewed on the hlidacstatu.cz website, MMR planned to give money for advertising in television, radio, press, the Internet, billboards or areas at railway stations through the state tourism support agency CzechTourism.
The largest share of the money, 700 million crowns, would be given to televisions.
The majority would be acquired by private TV Nova and Prima.
Printed diaries and weekly newspapers would get 400 million, and the same part would give MMR for PR articles, advertising banners and videos on the website.
Radio stations would receive 350 million for the media campaign, most of them private stations again.
The remaining 150 million would be used for outdoor advertising on billboards, LED screens and surfaces at railway stations or subways.
The campaign to promote tourism in the Czech Republic was supposed to start in mid-August and last for a year.
"The material is still beinging, it's definitely not yet in a form that should be presented," Dostálová said, without giving further details to the SeznamNews server.
According to the head of TOP 09 Markéta Pekarová Adamová, the media would profit from the advertising from the trust funds of Prime Minister Andrej Babiš (ANO).
"I firmly believe that this flamboyant contempt will be blown between the eyes in the autumn of the election," she tweeted.
Dostálová rejects the fact that it is really about helping the media affected by the crisis.
"It could also be a coincidence, I don't know anything at all about supporting the media," she told the Daily N on Friday.
According to her, a budget of two billion is indicative.
"We're still counting, of course, it could be less and more.
It's working material," she added.
Saving tourism doesn't make sense for a media campaign in support of domestic tourism.
"We want two billion for companies in tourism, not an unnecessary advertising campaign," Tomáš Barčík, chairman of the initiative, said on Sunday.
If there are two billion crowns in the state budget, it should be directed to companies, not to the media, the initiative writes in an open letter to the government.
Broth from life, rooster of beers and death like a blade of grass
Posthumous is also the third album titled A Death is a blade of the grass of the Brno poet, the tireless organizer of cultural activities and publisher Jaroslav Erik Frič, who also retired a year ago, on May 24.
In Frič’s discography it is the fifth item and, as with the previous titles, the main emphasis is on Frič’s poetry, of which he is himself an excellent performer.
Frič, the class of 1949, was, of course, influenced by the American beat generation, but this inspiration passed through his own, personally presented experiences and also very intrinsive expressions of faith.
However, he was distant from some agitation, when he talked or wrote about faith, about God, it was always very intimate, non-aggressive, completely tolerant.
In this context, Bohuslav Reynek (in contrast to Jan Zahradníček for example), the masters will forgive me.
Frič's album offers recordings of authorial reading from 2006, but also performances with musicians, who have always accompanied him very sensitively, whether it was the cellist Josef Klíč, or Honza Bartoň and Vítězslav Holata.
I'll help myself - gladly - in the words of Ostrava poet Petr Hruška about Frič's long poetic compositions.
"There was a proud and never-ending bitterness from them," Hruška writes, "over the state of our world threatened by extinction.
The world, increasingly sinking into dull consumerism and vulgarity, is increasingly moving away from what its soul was created - the mysteries of faith, humility, and humility.
There was a longing for him who knew that the values for which he had been created were of little interest, but who also knew that only because of general disinterest they could not be renounced.
The poems reminded us that you might have to lose everything expensive, but it's irrelevant how you're going to lose it.
If he sells himself to general decline, he resigns skeptically, silences fearfully, or remains until the very last moment in a calm and proud insistence on what he believes in and what he loves.”
Paroubek is not my adviser, Hamacek said
The former prime minister and chairman of the ČSSD, Jiří Paroubek, is not a paid adviser to Social Democracy.
The chairman of the party and Interior Minister Jan Hamáček only communicates with him and reads his opinions and articles.
Hamacek said this today on Prima Partie's television show.
On Friday, news server SeznamZpravy.cz reported that Paroubek should be Hamacek’s adviser.
"We'll text each other from time to time, he'll send me some material sometimes and I'll read it," Hamas said today.
He added, however, that this is not an official and paid cooperation.
According to him, the CSSD does not have the means to waste them for similar purposes.
"The fact that sometimes I read his opinion, there are a lot of people like that," he said.
In a subsequent discussion with ODS MP Jana Černochová, the minister said that many members of the ODS are still "unconceivingly listening to the opinions of Vaclav Klaus".
According to him, this is fine, because he is an experienced politician and a former party chairman, which is also the case of Paroubek.
The fact that the media is making an affair out of it marked this year's cucumber season.
News server SeznamZpravy.cz wrote on Friday that Paroubek is fulfilling the role of Hamacek's unofficial consultant.
He also wrote that Petr Benda, the former chairman of the local cell, who is affiliated with Paroubek, was heading for the fourth place of the party’s candidate in the Ústí region and he served as vice-chairman of his LEV 21.
Hamacek said today that he would not comment on the composition of the candidate until it was finished and approved.
Duplantis has a mile lead, it's a kick to the butt, lamented by Swede's challenger
The 22-year-old U.S
He already knows what it's likely to beat Duplantis two years younger, last year he overcame him at a meeting of NCAA universities.
"We had a battle the year before, that's when he won and I was second, then we switched.
In both cases it was a very good fight," describes Nilsen, who secured the victory with a performance of 5.95 meters.
But Duplantis has shot steeply upwards ever since, and he is currently alone in the tallest rod spheres.
At the age of just 20, he became a world record holder, surpassing the 6.17-metre bar in Toruni on February 8 this year, adding an inch a week later in Glasgow.
"He jumped six meters when he graduated from high school when he graduated from high school," his peer Nilsen recalled for the World Athletics website.
"It was clear that once he had matured from boy to man, all his potential and talent would tenfold.
Twenty-year is a pretty good age to jump the world record.”
Although Nilsen's list of achievements is much thinner than the one Duplantis prides himself on, an American sets similar goals.
So far he has hinted at NCAA races, which he regularly won, in February this year he improved his internal personal record to 5.93 m. Last August he won a gold medal at the Pan-American Games in Lima, already in 2017 he fought his way into the U.S. quest for the world championships, where he finished 13.
The closest milestone for him is for the first time to taste the Diamond League.
"It's definitely my priority to get to the professional circuit and race as much as possible in the Diamond League, because that's where you can make a name," he realizes.
"If you can succeed at this level, you can undoubtedly perform at events such as the Olympic Games, world championships or pan-American games."
Due to the pandemic, however, these peak holidays are postponed, which gives Nilsen and other sticks time to improve.
But will it be enough to catch up with Duplantis, who can easily turn a race under five circles into a mere battle for silver?
"Everyone's trying to catch up with him, but I don't think it's going to be possible anytime soon," Nilsen said.
"The kid's been jumping for 16 years and he's got a mile lead over everyone else in training."
And by the start of Tokyo 2021, he'll want to increase it further.
The woman spent hundreds of thousands on the disability pension of a helpless son.
She's about to face trial
The money that a 33-year-old man receives from the state as a disability pension is intended primarily to improve the quality of his life.
A woman faces up to eight years in prison for embezzlement.
Due to poor health, the court deprived the sick man of legal capacity and appointed his mother a guardian, who was also to manage his son's money.
"From mid-November 2013 to this January, a woman used part of the funds from her son's disability pension for her use and deprived him of 440 thousand crowns," police spokeswoman Dana Ladmanová said, adding that the accused woman was aggravated that, as a guardian, she had a special obligation to defend the interests of the injured party.
According to the instruction of the court, the woman set up an account for her son, but had his pension sent to her account.
"Following the payment of the costs associated withing a son's stay in a medical facility and the reimbursement of the necessary medicines, she used the remaining funds for the purchase of the vehicle, electrical appliances and for the operation of the household," the indictment reads.
That she can't use her son's money for her use, the woman admitted.
However, she defended herself by assisting them in the payment of family expenses.
"We've been having trouble tightening the running of the household.
We've never bought anything superior.
It's always been up to things whose purchase my son would approve.
I thought it was family money.
I didn't know I was committing a crime," the woman explained to police.
She promised to return the money to her son.
North America has been populated for 30 000 years, scientists say after finding tools
The finds from the Chiquihuite Cave in the Mexican state of Zacatas include 1930 limestone tools, including a fine blade that could have been used to slice meat, and pointed objects that may have served as spikes of spears or spears.
The researchers found that the oldest of the instruments came from 33 000 to 31 000 years ago.
“There are only a few products of this age.
But they're here," archaeologist Ciprian Ardelean of the University of Zacatecas and lead author of one of the two studies that were published on the subject was published in the journal Nature.
The youngest instruments reach an age of 12 500 years.
According to experts, the people of the Chiquihuite cave, located at a high altitude, have inhabited for 20 000 years.
So far, a widely accepted theory has indicated that the first representatives of Homo sapiens crossed the mainland bridge from today's Russia to Alaska about 13,500 years ago.
Archaeological evidence, including the unique spikes used to kill mammoths and other prehistoric animals, have showned that Clovian culture subsequently spread in much of North America.
However, in recent decades, this model received cracks when several ancient settlements were discovered, which were two to three thousand years older.
The second study, published in the journal Nature, describes 42 different sites throughout North America that prove the human presence on the continent between 26 000 and 19,000 years ago.
The researchers believe that a small number of people may have come to the North American mainland earlier than previously anticipated.
Some may have reached America by boat.
“It’s obvious that people have been in America long before the development of so-called Clovian culture,” said Ruth Gruhn, an anthropologist at the University of Alberta Province.
So far, interest in airline tickets is fulfilling an optimistic scenario, sellers say.
Prices are also gradually decreasing
Ticket sales in the Czech Republic are growing every week.
Compared to last summer, interest is still about half, but demand is growing by up to 20 percent every week.
According to the vendors, post-crisis development fulfills the most optimistic scenarios.
Ticket prices are also gradually decreasing.
One of the most sought-after destinations, according to retailers, is the southern European countries in particular.
According to Kiwi.com's data, they lead Spain, Italy and Greece.
At Letuška.cz, flights to Split, Croatia, are sold the most.
From other countries, Great Britain is the most sought-after, which has always been one of the busiest destinations.
The average ticket price also decreases.
At Letuška.cz, around 6250 crowns in the middle of the month, 3000 crowns less year-on-year.
This is mainly due to an increase in demand for European destinations, which now accounts for about 85 percent of sales.
However, prices are also falling to some European places, such as Spain and Italy have fallen by about a fifth in recent weeks.
The crisis has also affected the behaviour of customers, who are now buying tickets much less in advance than before because they are worried about the further development of the coronavirus situation.
According to Kiwi.com's earlier information, the time to buy the ticket before departure decreased by about ten days year-on-year.
"It confirms the future trend, when people will order tickets with much less in advance, will require more flexibility, less risk," Michal Šindelář, vice president for Kiwi.com products, said in a statement.
Currently, the average period of booking before departure is 21 days.
Along with the increase in sales, the number of renewed airlines from the Czech Republic is also increasing.
There are currently regular flights to more than 60 destinations at Prague Airport.
Compared to last year's summer season, this is about half destinations.
The grand finale tonight!
Watch darting battle live on Nova Sport
The second most prestigious tournament within the PDC organization reached the grand final.
It will feature experienced forty-nine-year-old Scottish dartster Gary Anderson and the surprise of the tournament, twenty-six-year-old Belgian Dimitri van den Bergh.
Gary Anderson knocked out Justin Pip in the first round, then bumped into his neighbor in the spreadsheet.
Even with James Wade, however, he handled each other, and in the quarter-finals he struggled with Simon Whitlock, who had previously sent home the world number one.
"Flying Scot" had almost in the semi-final when he faced Michael Smith.
At the last minute, however, he escaped the gravedigger from the shovel, dominated the extension and after the result of 18:16 he won a place in the final of the tournament, which he dominated already in 2018, when he defeated Mensur Suljovich 21:19 in an amazing battle.
The biggest surprise of World Matchplay is Dimitri van den Bergh.
Belgian talent enjoys the tournament, and just maybe the knowledge that he has nothing to lose has helped him to play on an absolutely fantastic wave.
The twenty-six-year-old dartsman first surprisingly knocked out world number six Nathan Aspinallo, overwhelmed Joe Cullen in a dramatic battle, and defeated Adrian Lewis in the quarter-finals.
The big clearance was the semi-finals.
Glen Durrant showed his strength at the outset, but calmly playing the Belgian got an excellent lead with an excellent series, which he then more or least easily kept until the end.
