Coronavirus cases have risen at least since the beginning of the week, the number of tests decreased
Since the beginning of March, when the new coronavirus was first discovered in the Czech Republic, 15 212 people have been infected with it.
Nearly 9,600 of them have already recovered, with 368 patients dying.
There are currently 5 254 people.
That's according to data on the Department of Health's website.
The daily number of newly confirmed cases this week has exceeded four times in a row two hundred.
The maximum was Friday's 281 cases.
On Thursday, 235 people tested positive, 246 a day earlier and 206 on Tuesday.
On the first working day of this week, there were 169 infected.
In the Czech Republic, 9 590 people have been cured of covid-19, or 63 percent of all those infected so far.
In most, the disease had a slight course.
According to the latest data from the Ministry of Health, there are 151 people with coronavirus in the hospital, the condition of 19 of them is assessed by doctors as serious.
There are several outbreaks of covid-19 in the Czech Republic.
Karvinsko still has the most infections in the last seven days per 100,000 inhabitants, almost 59.
This is followed by the district of Jihlava with 56 and Frýdecko-Místecko with 36 infected per 100 thousand inhabitants in the last week.
Due to the increased number of infections, new measures against the spread of coronavirus have been in place in the Czech Republic since Saturday.
People have to wear masks at indoor mass events above 100 people, these are cultural and sporting events, but also weddings and funerals inside buildings.
From Monday onwards, the capacity of these actions will be limited from the current 1,000 people in half.
In the world, the number of people who have proven to be infected with the new coronavirus has already exceeded the 16 million mark.
This was stated by Johns Hopkins University, which monitors the development of the pandemic globally.
According to this source, the death toll of victims who died with the covid-19 disease is approaching 645 thousand.
The newest million infected were added to the statistics in just three days; the 15-million mark fell on July 22.
In recent days, the United States, which has already registered more than 4.1 million infected, had the largest share of the rapid increase in the number of cases, Brazil with 2.4 million and India, where nearly 1.4 million people have been infected, according to authorities.
Infected people are now increasing at a record pace.
The World Health Organization announced this week that more than 284,000 new cases have been added globally from Thursday to Friday, the highest daily balance since the pandemic began.
This can be attributed to the fact that the spread of the disease is at its peak in populous states such as the U.S., Brazil and India, but according to Reuters, the number of new cases in the past week grew in nearly 40 countries around the world.
This applies, for example, to Australia, Japan, Bolivia, Sudan, Bulgaria, Belgium and Israel.
The outbreak has already occurred in almost all countries of the world, the first case today officially announced by the DPRK.
The air in households is up to five times worse than outdoors.
Air fresheners and deodorants are harmful to health
Health threatens not only poor air quality in outdoor areas, but also at home.
This was reported by the Israeli human health expert Carol Abraham, who was quoted by The Jerusalem Post.
According to Abraham, who was responsible for the creation of the Israeli Ministry of Environmental Protection, it is necessary to focus on household pollutants right now, when many people spend more time working from home.
Abraham referred to the research of the U.S. Environment Agency (EPA), according to which domestic air quality is up to five times worse than outdoors.
The World Health Organization (WHO) believes that 3.8 million people die prematurely every year due to poor air quality in their homes.
"Due to the coronavirus outbreak, which has forced many and is still forcing to work from home, people have to spend more hours in their homes than before, so it's time to focus on air quality in households," Ms Abraham said.
In Israel, almost no one deals with the quality of "home air".
Dangerous can be things that a person normally comes into contact with - coatings, air fresheners, disinfectants, deodorants or pesticides.
"Poisonous gas is also issued by the garments that we regularly carry to the dry cleaners," Ms Abraham said.
According to her, children, old members of the household and people with respiratory problems are most at risk.
Even otherwise healthy individuals may start suffering from problems such as headaches, fainting, nasal irritability, nosebleeds, vomiting or skin allergies.
With long-term exposure to unhealthy substances, internal organs and the central nervous system can be damaged.
However, not all pollutants come from objects inside households, the source can also be the material from which houses are built.
Vaclav Havel quiz: How long he spent in prison and what prompted him to write Charter 77
Fuenlabrada lost a chance to advance to Spain's top football league due to the coronavirus contagion.
The management of the competition canceled the originally postponed duel of the last round of the second league, in which the team was able to secure participation in the playoffs.
Fuenlabrada announced another 12 infected people on Saturday, bringing the total number of positive tests for covid-19 at the club as early as 28.
Due to contagion in the team, Monday's duel was postponed against La Coruni, in which Fuenlabrada was able to make it to the playoffs.
The management of the competition said today that the duel against La Coruni was definitively dismissed in view of force majeure due to the fact that there was no time to play it.
Fuenlabrada took the decision.
La Coruña, on the other hand, rebels against not playing the match.
In the end, due to the results of their rivals, it would not be enough to win over Fuenlabrada to save, but he finds it unfair that the last round did not take place according to customs complete in a unified term.
The management of the former elite Spanish club threatens a trial because of a fall in the third league.
The first two teams of the Huesca and Cádiz tables have a certainty of progressing among the elite.
Zaragoza, Almería, Girona and Elche will compete for the remaining third place in the playoffs.
Karel Gott didn't like Václav Havel?!
Daughter Dominica revealed it
"Dad didn't have him for many years," Gott's eldest daughter Dominica told Aha!, adding that the Master couldn't get Havel's name, and whenever he spoke to him, Karl was always upset.
It's hard to say what they've done, but according to Dominica, the dispute began sometime in the mid-1990s.
"Dad said in an article after the revolution that he had it easier than him.
That Havel could write in a drawer at the time of darkness, but if he defied himself somehow, he had an end to his career," Gottová said.
In the end, however, he adds that in the end, Czech giants found their way to each other.
"I know Havel couldn't have heared the word until his sixties.
But then the president congratulated him on his birthday letter, and all of a sudden Dad's relationship with him began to change," Dominika recalls.
Ten years later, Gott even invited the former president to celebrate his 70th birthday at the Ambassador Hotel on Wenceslas Square.
"And that's where a new era of their relationship began, because Mr. Havel's dad then regularly invited Mr. Havel to his house on Bertramka," added the blonde, whose father died last October.
Slavíka was also said to regret that he had never received state honours from Václav Havel.
After all, he got it in 2009, even though it was already out of the hands of his successor as Václav Klaus (79).
He will receive further honor in memoriam, the current president Miloš Zeman (75) is supposed to award the Order of the White Lion and honor his memory.
Heat in the Czech Republic from Tuesday, meteorologists warn of fires
The warning of meteorologists against high temperatures is valid for Tuesday afternoon for the whole of Bohemia, with the exception of the Karlovy Vary region and also for southern and central Moravia.
"Exceptionally, temperatures can exceed 34 degrees Celsius, especially in Central Bohemia," they say.
People are advised to observe a drinking regime in the heat and to reduce the body load in the noon and afternoon.
Due to the warm weather with a minimum of precipitation, there will be a risk of fires from Tuesday morning.
Windy weather could contribute to their rapid spread.
The warning of meteorologists about the risk of fires concerns Central Bohemia, including Prague, Liberec, Ústecký, Karlovy Vary and the Pilsen Region.
Warnings against fires will be in effect until further notice.
"Significant rainfall, which would mitigate the danger, in the northwest and west of Bohemia is not expected at least until the end of the week," the CHMI said.
The Prague magistrate responded to meteorologists' warnings by banning making fires in high-risk spots, which is scheduled to take effect from Tuesday.
People in the capital will not be able to start open fires, for example in parks, gardens or forests, up to 50 meters from the outskirts of the forest.
Smoking and the use of pyrotechnics are also prohibited in these places.
The measure will apply until further notice, Prague City Hall spokesperson Vít Hofman said.
